  "worked_solution": "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-2,1),$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-2,1].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-2,1]$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-2,1]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[2,3]$ and $[2,4]$ contain the point $x=3$ at which the function is not defined, so the function is not continuous on these intervals.",
